<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uprising of Asen and Peter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1185">1185</a>–<a href="/years/1187">1187</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uprising of Asen and Peter was the revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> against Byzantine rule, begun in 1185 by the brothers Theodore-Peter and Ivan Asen. Sparked by heavy taxation, it defeated several Byzantine expeditions and by 1187 had established the Second Bulgarian Empire, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uprising-of-asen-and-peter.jpg" alt="Uprising of Asen and Peter" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kandi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Second_Bulgarian_Empire_(1185-1196).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> then formed the Byzantine theme of Paristrion. To raise money for his wedding to the daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III of Hungary</a>, Emperor Isaac II Angelus levied a new tax that fell heavily on the people of the Haemus Mountains. They sent Peter and Asen to the emperor at Kypsella, now İpsala, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, asking to be enrolled in the Byzantine army and granted land near the Haemus to pay the tax. The request was refused and the brothers were treated roughly, so they threatened revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Many of their people were at first unwilling to rebel. The brothers built a church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> dedicated to Saint Demetrius, the patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, and claimed that the saint had abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. The revolt began on 26 October 1185, his feast day. The rebels attacked Byzantine cities, taking prisoners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and after a raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>, the old capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, Peter took the insignia of tsar.</p><p>Isaac's counter-offensive of spring 1186 at first succeeded: during the solar eclipse of 21 April 1186 his army defeated the rebels, many of whom fled north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> there and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> of the Pontic Steppe. Isaac then hurried back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and the rebels returned with Cuman allies to find the region undefended, taking the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>. Of the generals sent after them, John the sebastocrator and Alexius Branas each rebelled in turn, while John Kantakouzenos lost heavily in an ambush in the mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Isaac finally besieged the fortress of Lovech for three months without taking it. The lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Haemus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> were lost to the empire, and a truce recognised in practice the rule of Asen and Peter, creating the Second Bulgarian Empire under the Asen dynasty. The emperor kept as hostages Asen's wife and the brothers' younger brother John, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan of Bulgaria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uprising of Asen and Peter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
